Alsa: The Authentication Log Summarizer and Analyzer

DESCRIPTION
Alsa is a lightweight tool that turns authentication logs such as
auth.log into human-readable statistics and information while
offering automatic IP blocking via fail2ban.

CONFIGURATION
Alsa can be configured with its configuration file, named 'alsa.conf'.
Here, you can alter the values of the fields to fit your needs:

1. log-location: the name of the file to be parsed. Alsa will perform an absolute path
check to determine its actual location on the machine, so the value of this variable
should only be the name of the file, such as auth.log, and NOT /var/log/auth.log

2. num-failed-attempts-required: the number of failed password attempts required to 
perform an automatic fail2ban ban. If the number of blocks required is 0,
then automatic fail2ban banning will not be used. Otherwise, all IP
addresses recorded in the log file with associated failed login counts will be subject to 
being used in a fail2ban request, denying them any way to remotely login.

3. target-jail: the name of the jail that fail2ban will use when banning IP addresses.
You are free to name this whatever you want.

GENERAL USE
In the directory you have downloaded Alsa in, run the command: “python3 alsa.py”. 
Ensure that alsa.conf is in the same directory as the script is.
Alsa will automatically start parsing the authentication log and notify you when it has completed.
After each parse, Alsa will generate a structured JSON report titled "results.json" with
the list of all discovered IP addresses and the number of failed logins associated with each. 
The report is designed to be read and used in order to implement any additional security features if needed.
Alternatively, if num-failed-attempts-required is greater than 0, an autmoatic IP ban will 
be implemented using fail2ban.

